{"meta":{"ticker":"SPYM","as_of_date":"2026-06-30","performance_as_of":"2026-06-30","generated_at":"2026-07-01 08:23:25"},"benchmark":{"ticker":"SPY","fund_name":"State Street SPDR S&P 500 ETF"},"overview":{"ticker":"SPYM","fund_name":"State Street SPDR Portfolio S&P 500 ETF","category":"US Large-Cap Blend","issuer":"State Street","index_name":"S&P 500","region":"North America","exchange":"NYSEARCA","expense_ratio":0.0002,"aum":150412250000,"holdings_count":509,"inception_date":"2005-11-08","overall_score":9.38195996028244,"overall_rank":26,"sector_diversification_score":6.85171136909528},"description":{"tsi_description":"SPYM is State Street's low-cost ETF that passively tracks the S&P 500 Index. With a 0.02% expense ratio, it's the the cheapest S&P 500 Index on the market, and although it lacks the options market and liquidity characteristics of SPY, it's still liquid enough for the purposes of most retail investors. Mega-cap tech names are increasingly dominant in SPYM, and as a result, the ETF is geared toward investors whose primary goal is capital appreciation. Alternatives to SPYM include SPY, IVV, and VOO, with all acting as low-cost choices that adequately measure the performance of the U.S. stock market.","strategy_steps":["The S&P 500 Index is the large-cap division of the S&P 1500 Composite Index, which is comprised of 1500 of the largest U.S. stocks.","The Index does not necessarily hold the largest 500 U.S. stocks, since membership is determined by a Committee.","Among other requirements, new entrants must have positive GAAP net income from continuing operations for the most recent quarter and the sum of the most recent four consecutive quarters.","For equity REITs, Funds From Operations, or FFO, is used in place of GAAP net income.","The Index is rebalanced as needed, with each security's weight based on their free float market capitalization."],"best_for":["Core equity allocations","Growth-focused portfolios","Broad sector diversification"],"strength